\[y=\frac{1}{2}{\rm ln}\left(\frac{E+p_{z}}{E-p_{z}}\right), \tag{3.3}\]

where \(p_{z}\) is the parallel component of momentum along the beam direction. Rapidity is additive under Lorentz transformations: \(y\prime=y+a\), where \(y\prime\) is the rapidity in the lab frame, y is the rapidity in the center-of-mass frame. This means that the shape of the rapidity distribution is invariant under Lorentz transformations (see Appendix B). For ultrarelativistic particles, \(\beta\approx 1\) and \(E\approx p\) and rapidity can be approximated by pseudorapidity,

\[\eta=-{\rm lntan}(\frac{\theta}{2}) \tag{3.4}\]

where \(\theta=p_{z}/p\). We applied a cut on the pseudorapidity of the charged daughter tracks to be \(<1.2\), which is the pseudorapidity coverage of the silicon vertex detectors.

