**Background Free Equivalent - Effective Signal**

Effective signal refers to the significance in the absence of any background. In other words, \(S_{\rm eff}\) is the "background free equivalent". Significance of \(S_{\rm eff}\) is:

(D.7) \[\frac{S_{\rm eff}}{\delta S_{\rm eff}}=\frac{S_{\rm eff}}{\sqrt{S_{\rm eff}}} =\sqrt{S_{\rm eff}}.\]

Since we require that \(S_{\rm eff}\) has the same significance as in the case with background,

(D.8) \[\frac{S}{\delta S}=\frac{S_{\rm eff}}{\sqrt{S_{\rm eff}}},\]

(D.9) \[\frac{S}{\sqrt{S+2B}}=\sqrt{S_{\rm eff}}.\]

Therefore we obtain \(S_{\rm eff}\) as

(D.10) \[S_{\rm eff}=\frac{S}{2\frac{B}{S}+1}.\]

This is the signal strength in the absence of any background that has same significance as a measurement with background. Quoting \(S_{\rm eff}\) avoids asking what is the signal-to-background ratio. Another advantage is optimization of cuts. Sometimes tuning cuts to reduce background also reduces the signal. Since \(S_{\rm eff}\) reflects both signal and background, it's the simplest measure to judge the optimization study.

